  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nCurrent-Saturday... A pocket of deeper moisture (PWAT ~1.8-2.0\") has \nsupported persistent rounds of showers across the Treasure Coast and \nOkeechobee county today. Further north, coverage of showers has \nremained more isolated, still managing to reach areas far inland and \nwest of the Orlando Metro at times. Most guidance points to a \ndiminishing trend across areas north of Cape Canaveral and the \nOrlando Metro late this afternoon while rounds of at least isolated \nshowers should generally persist along portions of the Treasure \nCoast into this evening and overnight. While not as breezy as recent \ndays, wind gusts of 20-25 mph have been observed across portions of \neast central Florida today. The pressure gradient tightens on \nSaturday as high pressure further builds over the eastern U.S. Winds \nrespond, with gusts increasing 25-30 mph at times into Saturday \nafternoon. The deepest moisture should generally persist across \nsouthern portions of the forecast area early this weekend with best \nchances for rounds of onshore moving showers (~40-60%) continuing \nmostly south of Cape Canaveral tomorrow. Lightning chances remain \n20% or less, and strong storms are not expected. Cloudy skies \ncontinue with high temperatures ranging the low to mid 80s.\n\nHazardous conditions persists at area beaches. A high risk of life \nthreatening rip currents continues, and entering the water is not \nadvised. While a period of high astronomical tides continue, P-ETTS \nguidance keeps Saturday morning's high tide cycle below Minor flood \nstage, and the Coastal Flood Advisory has been allowed to expire \nfrom Indian River northward. However, will maintain the Coastal \nFlood Advisory to include Saturday afternoon's high tide cycle along \nthe St. Lucie and Martin coast as guidance at the Lake Worth Pier \nmay suggest the potential for Moderate coastal flood impacts to \ncontinue.\n\nSunday-Monday... Ridging aloft breaks down on Sunday as a mid level \ndisturbance approaches and passes over central Florida. Moisture \nincreases late in the weekend as a stalled surface boundary over \nsouth Florida begins to lift northward. Rain chances (60-80%) \nnotably increase late in the day Sunday, continuing through the \novernight and into Monday. Ensemble and probabilistic guidance has \npointed to the potential for a heavy rainfall threat as rain chances \nincrease, and multiple ensemble suites suggest average 24 hour \nprecip totals exceeding 1-2 inches across much of east central \nFlorida. A Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall exists areawide \nSunday and Sunday night, and will continue to keep an eye on areas \nof southern Brevard and the Treasure Coast where soils remain \nsaturated and locally higher QPF totals are currently modeled.\n\nBeyond the rainfall forecast, expect breezy and gusty onshore winds \nto persist on Sunday before slackening just a little on Monday. \nIncreased cloudiness and precip will hold temperatures near seasonal \nvalues in the upper 70s and low 80s each day.\n\nTuesday-Friday (previous)... Heavy rain potential abates beginning \nTuesday, but 30-50% shower chances linger for the middle of the week \nas sufficient moisture remains in place. More intervals of sun \nshould return, and winds become noticeably lighter by Wednesday. \nThis will allow high temps to warm back into the low and mid 80s for \nthe middle of the week.\n\nA cold front is forecast to reach Central Florida around Thursday. \nIndications are that the front will be weakening on approach, but it \nmay squeeze out a band of showers and a few storms. Severe weather \npotential currently appears limited.\n\nDrier and perhaps slightly cooler air drifts toward the state behind \nthe front on Friday. The less humid conditions should make for \ncooler nighttime conditions, but afternoons still look warm.",
